//? Title: A Test Document
//? Import: opts.mepml
//? Style: test.mepss

\abstract(
This document exercises *every* construct mepml has, from ~inline markup~ and
$e^{i\pi} + 1 = 0$ to executable code blocks and the results they write back.

An abstract can run over several paragraphs; a blank line separates them.
)

> Analysis
//? Import: refs.mepml
//? MACRO: Let's worry about this one later...

\toc

// This is a comment. The above options can be int, double, or string valued

// Space between text (i.e. new line characters on their own line) indicates new paragraphs.

\import(refs.mepml)
// NOTE: this doesnt need to be a mepml file, but does need to be compatible.
// also note, the above syntax for NOTE: is special, that allow for special
// formatting of notes. There are others by default WARNING, ERROR, INFO, and
// others that might make sense here

> Heading level 1

Here, we have some _underlined_ text and we have some *bold text* and we have
some `verbatim text` and we have some [my website|https://yabla.com]. and here
we have some ~italic text~ and we have some ^superscript^ and we have some
,,subscript,, and we have some <small text> and we have some >big text< and we
have some |monospaced text| and we have some =highlighted text= and we have some
-strikethrough text- and we have some +inserted text+ and we have some !deleted
text!. Here's some \f(Helvetica, \fs(12, Helvetica, font size 12)) text and
here's some \fs(24, font size 24) text. Here's some \color(red, red text) and
here's some \color(blue, blue text) . Here's some \fs(16, \color(red, font size
16 red text)). Here's a \fn(footnote here). And here's a citation: \cite(asdf1),
and here's a parenthetical citation: \citep(asdf1).

Here's some latex math: here's inline $E=mc^2$ and here's some display math:
$$
  \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} e^{-x^2} dx = \sqrt{\pi}
$$
\alttext(Some alternative text describing the equation)

Other syntax works too like this inline \(\frac{a}{b}\)\alttext(More alt text) and this display math
\[ 
  \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{1}{n^2} = \frac{\pi^2}{6} 
\]

Note that we can optionally include

// Some comment about the code block to come
