A dividing line is 3 or more horizontal characters (any one of - = _ *)

=================

> Additions beyond the original sketch

// NOTE: everything below this heading was added while implementing the
// parser; see help/mepml.org for the full reference.

// INFO: subscript is `,,x,,` rather than `_x_` because `_x_` is already underline.

// WARNING: a line starting with `>` and a space is always a heading, so
// `>big<` text at the very start of a line must not have a space after the `>`.

Lists use -, * or + for bullets and 1. or 1) for numbers; indent to nest:

- first item with *bold* inside
- second item
  - a nested item
  - [ ] an open task
  - [x] a finished task
1. numbered
2. and another

Commands are `\name(arguments, text)`: the arguments end at the first commas,
so the text may hold commas of its own, and an unmatched parenthesis in it
takes a backslash, \). The older \fs{12}{braced} spelling still reads, as
do the @image{...}, @caption{...} and other @name{...} directives; only the
abstract must be written \abstract(...).

Escape any marker with a backslash: \*not bold\*, \$5 and \$6, \\. A lone
dollar amount like $5 is never math. Inline text // a trailing comment is hidden.

Tables can align columns with colons in the separator row, and the pipes
don't need to line up in the source (mep lines the columns up when drawing):

| Left | Centre | Right |
| :--- | :----: | ----: |
| a | b | 1 |
| `c` | *d* | 2.50 |
| a longer cell | >big< | 100 |
\caption(An aligned table)

GitHub-flavoured Markdown tables work as they are. After a header and a
delimiter row, the outer pipes are optional, and \| is a pipe inside a cell:

Species | Petal length | Petal width
:------ | -----------: | ----------:
~setosa~ | 1.46 | 0.25
versicolor | 4.26 | 1.33
| virginica | 5.55 | 2.03 |
\caption(A Markdown table)

Code blocks take options in the header or from //? lines right above them,
and a ```lang header works too. Results are written back between the markers
when the block is run (C-c C-c in mep):

```{python, eval=true}
print(sum(range(10)))
```

```r
x <- 1:10
mean(x)
```

A block with a file= option draws a figure: R and Python have their plotting
pointed at that file, and after a run the image is shown in the block's
results, numbered and captioned like any other figure:

```{r, file="squares.png"}
x <- 1:10
plot(x, x^2, type = "b", main = "Squares")
```
\caption(Squares, drawn by R)
\alttext(A line plot of x squared for x from 1 to 10)

A block with results=html prints HTML: its results keep that markup, and mep
draws it in place of the text (move the cursor into it to see the markup).
The exports embed it as HTML:

```{python, results=html}
rows = [("apple", 3), ("banana", 5), ("cherry", 12)]
print("<p><b>Fruit</b> counted by <i>Python</i>:</p>")
print("<table>")
print("<tr><th>Fruit</th><th>Count</th></tr>")
for name, n in rows:
    print(f"<tr><td>{name}</td><td>{n}</td></tr>")
print("</table>")
```
// result_begin: html
// <p><b>Fruit</b> counted by <i>Python</i>:</p>
// <table>
// <tr><th>Fruit</th><th>Count</th></tr>
// <tr><td>apple</td><td>3</td></tr>
// <tr><td>banana</td><td>5</td></tr>
// <tr><td>cherry</td><td>12</td></tr>
// </table>
// result_end

A block with results=markdown prints Markdown, which is written out as it
is and read as part of the document: a table it prints is a table, numbered
and captioned like any other. (R's knitr::kable() prints one.)

```{python, results=markdown}
rows = [("apple", 7), ("banana", 5)]
print("| Fruit | Count |")
print("|:------|------:|")
for name, n in rows:
    print(f"| {name} | {n} |")
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Fruit | Count |
|:------|------:|
| apple | 7 |
| banana | 5 |
// result_end
\caption(Fruit, counted by Python)

The exports show each block's code and its results. `exports=` (code,
results, both or none) or knitr's `echo=false` (results only) changes that for
one block, and an `Exports:` line in the header for every block. The editor
always shows everything; this block appears in no export:

```{r, exports=none}
cat("only in the editor")
```
// result_begin:
// only in the editor
// result_end

An exec block runs a program in a terminal inside its results: C-c C-c starts
it, Enter (or a click) types into it, Ctrl-\ Ctrl-N comes back to the text,
and C-c C-k (or the stop button) stops it. When it ends, its last screen is
kept as the results:

```{exec, rows=8}
python3 -q
```
// result_begin: terminal
// >>> print(6*7, [c.upper() for c in "mep"])
// 42 ['M', 'E', 'P']
// >>>
// result_end

With results=exec, a block in any language is the program: it is compiled
(when the language is) and then run in the same kind of terminal:

```{c, results=exec, rows=6}
#include <stdio.h>
int main(void) {
    char name[64];
    printf("What is your name? ");
    fflush(stdout);
    if (fgets(name, sizeof name, stdin)) printf("Hello, %s", name);
    return 0;
}
```
// result_begin: terminal
// What is your name? Jordan
// Hello, Jordan
// result_end

An exec-gui block runs a program with a window of its own, and shows that
window inside the results (on X11): Enter or a click hands it the keyboard,
Ctrl-\ takes it back, and when it closes its last picture is kept:

```{exec-gui, rows=12}
xeyes
```
// result_begin: gui
// [running] inkscape
// result_end

```{python, results=exec-gui, rows=10}
import math
import tkinter as tk

root = tk.Tk()
root.title("smile")
canvas = tk.Canvas(root, bg="black", highlightthickness=0)
canvas.pack(fill="both", expand=True)

def draw(event=None):
    canvas.delete("all")
    w, h = canvas.winfo_width(), canvas.winfo_height()
    a = min(w * 0.38, h * 1.6)  # half the smile's width
    b = h * 0.4                 # how deep it curves
    cx, cy = w / 2, h * 0.18
    line = max(4, h * 0.03)

    def lip(x):  # the smile's height at x
        return cy + b * math.sqrt(max(0.0, 1 - ((x - cx) / a) ** 2))

    # The tongue, out of the smile halfway to its right end, leaning that way.
    tx = cx + a / 2
    tw, tl = h * 0.24, h * 0.34
    lean = tw * 0.35
    left, right = tx - tw / 2, tx + tw / 2
    pts = [left, lip(left), left, lip(left),
           left + lean * 0.2, lip(tx) + tl * 0.7,
           tx + lean * 0.6, lip(tx) + tl,
           right + lean, lip(tx) + tl * 0.62,
           right, lip(right), right, lip(right)]
    canvas.create_polygon(pts, smooth=True, fill="white", outline="white")
    canvas.create_line(tx + lean * 0.05, lip(tx) + line * 1.5, tx + lean * 0.35, lip(tx) + tl * 0.55,
                       fill="black", width=max(2, line * 0.4), capstyle="round")
    # The smile, drawn over the tongue's root.
    canvas.create_arc(cx - a, cy - b, cx + a, cy + b, start=190, extent=160,
                      style="arc", outline="white", width=line)

canvas.bind("<Configure>", draw)
root.mainloop()
```
// result_begin:
// \image(test-gui-7481ee16.png)
// [stopped]
// result_end

Slides: `\slide(` on a line of its own, then the slide's content -- any
blocks at all -- and a line holding just `)` to end it (`\slide{` ... `}`
and `@slide{` ... `}` work too). A slide's first heading is its title.

\slide(
>> A slide
- Its points are ordinary blocks
- *Formatting* works as it does anywhere else
)

@slide{
Two slides in a row, this one untitled.
}

Boxes: `\definition(Title,` on a line of its own, then the box's content --
any blocks -- and a line holding just `)` to close it. `\theorem`, `\lemma`,
`\proposition`, `\corollary`, `\fact`, `\example`, `\remark` and `\proof`
work the same way, each in its own colour. The title is everything before
the first comma; text after it on the same line starts the box's first
paragraph, and a box closed where its text ends needs no line of its own.

\definition(Orthogonal projection,
The point of a subspace $S$ closest to $\mathbf{y}$:

$$
\hat{\mathbf{y}} = P\mathbf{y}, \qquad P = X (X^\top X)^{-1} X^\top
$$

- $P$ is *symmetric*
- $P^2 = P$
)

\theorem(Pythagoras, If $\mathbf{a} \perp \mathbf{b}$, the squared lengths
add up: $\| \mathbf{a} + \mathbf{b} \|^2 = \| \mathbf{a} \|^2 + \| \mathbf{b} \|^2$.
)

\proof(
Expand the square; the cross term is $2\, \mathbf{a}^\top \mathbf{b} = 0$.

\remark(Nested, Boxes nest, each closing before the one around it.)
)

\example(No comma means no title: this box is all text.)

User commands: `\define(name(params), template)` makes a command of your
own, and `\name(args)` calls it -- the template with `#param` replaced by
the arguments. A template (or any text) can choose what each export gets
with `\when(formats, text)` and `\otherwise(text)`, and write an export's
own markup with `\raw(formats, text)`.

\define(glossary(term, body),
\raw(html, <div class="definition">)

*Definition (#term).* #body

\raw(html, </div>)
)

\glossary(Literate programming, Prose and code in one document, the code
run and its results kept beside it.)

This paragraph was exported as \when(html, a web page) \otherwise(anything
but a web page)\raw(tex, \footnote{typeset by \LaTeX}).

\raw(tex, \clearpage)

How all of this looks is not part of the document: the header's `Style:`
line names a style sheet, `test.mepss` beside this file, whose rules
restyle what mep's default sheet draws -- here the level-3 headings, the
bullets and the colour of a remark. See `docs/mepml-spec/`.

\remark(A sheet is CSS in syntax,
`box[kind=remark] { --accent: theme(Purple, #6a51a3); }` is the rule that
colours this box.
)

A sheet can be written here too, as a `raw` block for the format `style`
(no export has it, so the block is in none of them). It gives a look to
two things that have none of their own: a box of a kind of this document's
own, and text with a class.

\raw(style,
box[kind=axiom] { --accent: theme(Orange, #b35900); }
.term { color: theme(Green, #2f7d32); font-weight: bold; }
)

\boxed(axiom, Extensionality,
Two sets with the same \class(term, elements) are the same set.
)

A bibtex-style entry works as well (so a .bib file can be @imported):

Knuth coined the term literate programming \citep(knuth84).

\bibliography
